# RESERVED — migration numbers claimed by an in-flight lane branch
#
# Owner ruling PARALLEL-WRITERS `0464ad54` §2 (2026-09-03) replaced the
# one-product-writer rule with parallel lane branches, and with it the
# assumption that migration numbers on `main` are contiguous:
#
#   > "Migration numbers are RESERVED here, not read live. A lane uses ONLY
#   >  its reserved numbers; an unreserved number is a packet violation. ...
#   >  The migration runner orders by filename, so a reserved number that
#   >  integrates LATER than a higher one is acceptable ONLY if the files are
#   >  independent (a lane's migration must not depend on another lane's
#   >  schema; if it does, say so in the handover and integrate after it)."
#
# Because lanes integrate serially in a preference order that is NOT the
# numeric order, `main` legitimately holds a GAP wherever a lower reserved
# number belongs to a lane that has not integrated yet. This file is the
# authority on which gaps are legitimate: `projectResourceMigrationContract`
# asserts that every absent number past the 067 tip appears HERE, so an
# unreserved gap — a renumbering, a deleted migration, a typo'd filename —
# still fails the gate exactly as it did before the ruling.
#
# A lane that needs a number not listed here asks the dispatcher and this file
# gains a line FIRST.
#
# THIS LEDGER IS APPEND-ONLY HISTORY. A line is NEVER removed once written,
# not even after its migration has integrated (review `bfac1dd5` finding F4).
# Retiring lines was the defect: with the line gone, a DELETED migration looked
# like an ordinary un-integrated reservation and the gate stayed green. Keeping
# every line means the gate can tell "not written yet" from "was here and is
# gone", because the second question is answered by git, not by this file.
#
# The gate that reads this file
# (`backend/src/__tests__/projectResourceMigrationContract.test.ts`) enforces:
#   1. every migration number PRESENT past the 067 tip is reserved HERE — an
#      unreserved new migration is a packet violation, not a free choice;
#   2. every number ABSENT in the range is reserved here — a renumbering fails;
#   3. every migration file present at the MERGE BASE is still present at HEAD
#      — deletion is caught by git, an anchor this file cannot forge;
#   4. this file's grammar is complete and no number is reserved twice.
#
# Format: <number> <lane-branch-or-lane-name> [<card>]
